elaine: Heads-up: hit a weird goroutine leak in the Go SDK generator when token refresh happens mid-stream and the request context gets cancelled. I have a tiny repro in sampleapp/go/generator/leak.go. Anyone seen similar?

sam: Yep, ran into the same pattern in a customer integration last week. Handshake + midstream auth refresh seems to produce a race: the stream reconnect code doesn't fully stop the old goroutine.

mike: Can you paste the minimal repro? curious how you're creating the ctx and wiring the stream.

elaine: Sure — simplified snippet below. It spawns a generator stream and cancels ctx on signal. The leak appears when the auth refresh triggers a 401 and we cancel around the same time.

```go
ctx, cancel := context.WithCancel(context.Background())
defer cancel()

req := inference.GeneratorRequest{
  Prompt: "streaming test",
  MaxTokens: 500,
}
stream, err := client.Generator.Stream(ctx, req)
if err != nil {
  log.Fatal(err)
}

go func() {
  // reads until stream.Close or ctx canceled
  for stream.Next() {
    fmt.Println(stream.Event())
  }
  if err := stream.Err(); err != nil {
    log.Printf("stream err: %v", err)
  }
}()

// simulate signal after auth refresh
time.Sleep(1500 * time.Millisecond)
cancel()
```

jen: A couple of things here: 1) Go SDK parity note — Node/Python have explicit Resume/Abort semantics on the generator handle. Go's current streaming API leans on ctx cancellation only, which makes graceful shutdown ambiguous. 2) If we want to support transparent reconnect/resume on auth refresh it's tricky — we need a resume token and idempotency guarantees.

sam: IMO we should avoid automatic reconnect/resume inside the SDK for now. Prefer explicit API: user gets an error event like `auth:expired` with an optional resume_token. App can decide to call `Resume(resume_token)` or give up. That keeps behavior predictable and matches other SDKs' eventual direction.

mike: Workaround for now (small patch): create a stream wrapper that injects an internal cancel when you see a retryable auth error, and then expose a boolean `Abandoned` flag so caller knows not to attempt to read anymore. Quick pseudo-fix:

```go
// inside sdk stream reader
select {
case <-ctx.Done(): // user canceled
  close(done)
case err := <-authErrCh: // auth refresh failed
  s.abandoned.Store(true)
  close(done)
}
```

elaine: That helps. The hard part was duplicate callbacks: sometimes the resumed stream delivers the last chunk again and apps that append become duped. If we add resume_token to events, we should document idempotency expectations.

jen: Agree. Proposal: add explicit `Abort()` and `Resume(token string)` methods to GeneratorStream handle in Go, plus an `AuthExpired` event that includes `resume_token`. Update sampleapp/generator to show correct pattern. That should reduce onboarding friction.

ci-bot: :robot_face: nightly-ci failed: generator_stream_resume_test flaked (logs at https://ci/internal/12345). Repro run attached.

sam: Good catch CI — the flaky test is exactly the race we're discussing. I'll pin a skip and add a TODO to re-enable after the API change.

mike: I'll put up a minimal PR for the wrapper that avoids the leak today. It won't add Resume, but will stop goroutines reliably on ctx cancel.

elaine: Thanks — I'll extract the repro into a deterministic unit test and post it in the PR comments. Also adding a note in the onboarding guide about "don't rely on transparent resume" to reduce surprise for new users.

jen: I'll own the API design doc: will sketch the Go ergonomics + examples (Abort vs Cancel, Resume semantics, idempotency notes) and sync with Node/Python owners for parity.

elaine: Action items: jen -> API doc; mike -> leak-fix PR; sam -> temp test skip + CI ticket; elaine -> deterministic repro and sampleapp update. Thanks all. :thumbsup:
